k_2-3=0\] \[k_2=3\] Hence, \[k_1=0,\qquad k_2=3,\qquad k_3=2\] Therefore, option B is correct. \[\boxed{\text{(b) 0.0,\;3.0,\;2.0}}\]

Initially, all the internal states and inputs of the 4-bit ripple carry adder are reset to \(0\). At \(t = 0\), the inputs are changed to: \[X_3X_2X_1X_0 = 1100\] \[Y_3Y_2Y_1Y_0 = 0100\] \[Z_0 = 1\] Let us first analyze the inputs and observe where a transition occurs, as we only need to study the outputs where the state changes from \(0\) to \(1\): <img src="img/fc40f4876609c839.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)"> As observed from the block diagram, the state transitions occur only for \(S_0\), \(Z_3\), and \(Z_4\). We can analyze the timing for each of these outputs one by one: <strong>1. For Sum \(S_0\)</strong> At the first full adder (\(\text{FA}_0\)), the inputs are \(X_0 = 0\), \(Y_0 = 0\), and \(Z_0 = 0 \rightarrow 1\). Looking at the circuit diagram of Figure II: <img src="img/7211f986beffa1f0.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)"> Since \(X_0 = 0\) and \(Y_0 = 0\), the output of \(\text{XOR}_1\) remains \(0\), bypassing its propagation delay. The transition from \(Z_0\) propagates solely through \(\text{XOR}_2\) to reach \(S_0\). Thus, \(S_0\) stabilizes after a single \(\text{XOR}\) gate delay: \[t_{S0} = 20\,\text{ns}\] <strong>2. For Carry \(Z_3\)</strong> At the third full adder (\(\text{FA}_2\)), the inputs are \(X_2 = 1\) and \(Y_2 = 1\), while the incoming carry is \(Z_2 = 0 \rightarrow 0\) (stable at \(0\,\text{ns}\)). <img src="img/9d5dafaf648708d1.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)"> The carry output \(Z_3\) transitions to \(1\) as soon as any input to the final \(\text{OR}\) gate becomes \(1\). Given \(X_2 = 1\) and \(Y_2 = 1\): <ul><li>Output of \(\text{AND}_1\) becomes \(1\) after a propagation delay of \(15\text{ ns}\).</li><li>This signal travels through the final \(\text{OR}\) gate, adding a delay of \(10\text{ ns}\).</li></ul> Thus, \(Z_3\) stabilizes at: \[t_{Z_3} = 15\text{ ns} + 10\text{ ns} = 25\text{ ns}\] <strong>3. For Carry \(Z_4\)</strong> At the fourth full adder (\(\text{FA}_3\)), the inputs are \(X_3 = 0\) and \(Y_3 = 1\), and the incoming carry transitions as \(Z_3 = 0 \rightarrow 1\) at \(t = 25\,\text{ns}\). <img src="img/d58f11bc55755c90.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)"> The carry propagation path passes through the \(\text{AND}_2\) gate, which requires two inputs: the output of \(\text{XOR}_1\) (\(X_3 \oplus Y_3\)) and the incoming carry \(Z_3\). <ul><li>\(\text{XOR}_1\) output settles at \(t = 20\text{ ns}\).</li><li>Carry input \(Z_3\) arrives at \(t = 25\text{ ns}\).</li></ul> The \(\text{AND}_2\) gate must wait for the later of the two concurrent inputs to arrive: \[t_{\text{inputs}} = \max(20\text{ ns}, 25\text{ ns}) = 25\text{ ns}\] Accounting for the subsequent gate propagation delays (\(\text{AND}_2 = 15\text{ ns}\), \(\text{OR} = 10\text{ ns}\)): \[\begin{aligned}
    t_{\text{AND}_2\text{ out}} &amp;= 25\text{ ns} + 15\text{ ns} = 40\text{ ns} \\
    t_{Z_4} &amp;= 40\text{ ns} + 10\text{ ns} = 50\text{ ns}
\end{aligned}\] Thus, the maximum time delay for \(Z_4\) to become stable is \(50\text{ ns}\). Out of all the internal and external transitions occurring across the entire circuit, the maximum delay is \(50\,\text{ns}\). Therefore, the output of the ripple carry adder will be completely stable at \(t = 50\,\text{ns}\). \[\boxed{50}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>In parallel or ripple-carry structures, intermediate signals propagate simultaneously along different paths.</li><li>When a logic gate requires multiple changing inputs to compute its stable output, its timing is constrained by the maximum (\(\max\)) arrival time among those necessary inputs.</li><li>The overall settling time of a combinational circuit is determined by the longest critical propagation delay path from any input to any output.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not blindly multiply the single-stage full adder delay by the total number of bits (\(4 \times \text{delay}\)) to find the maximum path delay. Specific input transitions can shortcut or parallelize the carry path.</li><li>Avoid adding up delays of gates whose inputs do not undergo any transition during the cycle.</li></ul></div>

Given parameters from the problem statement: \[\begin{aligned}
    \text{Clock frequency } (f) &amp;= 5\,\text{MHz} = 5 \times 10^6\,\text{Hz} \\
    \text{Execution time } (t) &amp;= 1.4\,\mu\text{s} = 1.4 \times 10^{-6}\,\text{s}
    \end{aligned}\] The duration of a single T-state (\(T\)) corresponds to one clock period: \[T = \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{5 \times 10^6\,\text{Hz}} = 0.2 \times 10^{-6}\,\text{s} = 0.2\,\mu\text{s}\] The total instruction execution time is given by the product of the number of T-states (\(N\)) and the duration of one T-state: \[t = N \times T\] Substituting the known values to find \(N\): \[1.4\,\mu\text{s} = N \times 0.2\,\mu\text{s}\] \[N = \frac{1.4\,\mu\text{s}}{0.2\,\mu\text{s}} = 7\] Hence, the number of T-states needed for executing the instruction is \(7\). \[\boxed{\text{(C)7}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A T-state represents the basic timing unit of an 8085 microprocessor, equal to one complete clock cycle.</li><li>\(\text{Execution Time} = (\text{Total Number of T-states}) \times (\text{Clock Period})\).</li></ul></div>

For unity feedback, the characteristic equation (CE) is \(1 + G(s) = 0\): \[s^3 + ks^2 + 2s + 1 + 2s + 2 = 0 \implies s^3 + ks^2 + 4s + 3 = 0\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Constructing the Routh array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc} 
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 4 \\ 
s^2 &amp; k &amp; 3 \\ 
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{4k-3}{k} &amp; 0 \\ 
s^0 &amp; 3 &amp; 
\end{array}\] For the system to oscillate, the \(s^1\) row must be zero: \[\dfrac{4k-3}{k} = 0 \implies 4k - 3 = 0\] \[k = 0.75\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>For a \(3^{rd}\) order system \(as^3 + bs^2 + cs + d = 0\) to be marginally stable: \[\text{Inner Product} = \text{Outer Product}\] \[bc = ad\] Substituting \(a=1, b=k, c=4, d=3\): \[(k)(4) = (1)(3)\] \[k = \dfrac{3}{4} = 0.75\]</div> \[\boxed{0.75}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div>The frequency of oscillation \(\omega\) is derived from the auxiliary equation: \(A(s) = ks^2 + 3 = 0 \implies \omega = \sqrt{3/k}\).</div>

Given, \[G(s)=\frac{10K(s+2)}{s^3+3s^2+10}
\quad \text{and} \quad
0&lt;K&lt;1\] The Nyquist plot of \(G(s)\) is already provided in the question. For unity negative feedback, the characteristic equation is \[1+G(s)=0\] The poles of the closed-loop transfer function are the zeros of the characteristic equation. Applying Nyquist criterion, \[N=P_{+}-Z_{+}\] where, <ul><li>\(N\) = net number of encirclements of \((-1,j0)\)</li><li>\(P_{+}\) = number of right half plane poles of characteristic equation, which is same as the poles of open loop system</li><li>\(Z_{+}\) = number of right half plane zeros of characteristic equation, which are same as the poles of closed loop system</li></ul> From the given Nyquist path, the contour is traversed in clockwise direction. Now, for \(0&lt;K&lt;1\) the point \(-K\) lies between \(0\) and \(-1\) on the real axis. From the Nyquist plot, the critical point \((-1,j0)\) is not encircled. Hence, \[N=0\] Now, let us determine \(P_{+}\). Open-loop transfer function denominator is \[s^3+3s^2+10=0\] Routh array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\ s^2 &amp; 3 &amp; 10 \\ s^1 &amp; -\dfrac{10}{3} &amp; 0 \\ s^0 &amp; 10 &amp; \\
\end{array}\] First column elements are \[1,\quad 3,\quad -\frac{10}{3},\quad 10\] There are two sign changes. Hence, \[P_{+}=2\] Substituting in Nyquist criterion, \[0=2-Z_{+}\] \[Z_{+}=2\] \[\boxed{\text{(C) } 2}\]

