Let \(X(t)\) be a wide sense stationary random process with the power spectral density \(S_X(f)\) as shown in Figure (a), where \(f\) is in Hertz (Hz). The random process \(X(t)\) is input to an ideal lowpass filter with the frequency response \[H(f)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; |f| \leq \dfrac{1}{2}\ \text{Hz} \\
0, &amp; |f| &gt; \dfrac{1}{2}\ \text{Hz}
\end{cases}\]as shown in Figure (b). The output of the lowpass filter is \(Y(t)\). <img src="img/52f8a49da33afebf.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> <img src="img/51e8e74767d8f7d3.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> <br/>Let \(E\) be the expectation operator and consider the following statements: <ol><li>\(E\big(X(t)\big)=E\big(Y(t)\big)\)</li><li>\(E\big(X^2(t)\big)=E\big(Y^2(t)\big)\)</li><li>\(E\big(Y^2(t)\big)=2\)</li></ol>Select the correct option:

questions.explanation:
(I) \(E[x(t)] = E[y(t)]\) For an LTI system, the output mean is scaled by the DC gain \(H(0)\). \[E[y(t)]
=
H(0)\,E[x(t)]\]Given \(H(0)=1\), \[E[y(t)]
=
E[x(t)]\]Hence, \[\boxed{
E[x(t)]=E[y(t)]
}\] (II) \(E[x^2(t)] \ne E[y^2(t)]\) <strong>Proof:</strong> Average power of a WSS random process is obtained from the area under its PSD. \[E[y^2(t)]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}S_y(f)\,df\]For an LTI system, \[S_y(f)=S_x(f)|H(f)|^2\]Therefore, \[E[y^2(t)]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}S_x(f)|H(f)|^2\,df\] \[=
\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}S_x(f)\,df\] \[=
2-2e^{-0.5}
\qquad \cdots (1)\]Now, \[E[x^2(t)]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}S_x(f)\,df\] \[=2
\qquad \cdots (2)\]Since, \[(1)\ne(2)\]Hence, \[\boxed{
E[x^2(t)]\ne E[y^2(t)]
}\] (III) \(E[y^2(t)] \ne 2\) <strong>Proof:</strong> From part (II), \[E[x^2(t)]=2\]but, \[E[y^2(t)]
=
2-2e^{-0.5}
\ne2\]Hence, \[\boxed{
E[y^2(t)]\ne2
}\] \[\boxed{
\text{(A) only I is true}
}\]

Four seats around a square table: North, East, South, West. Given constraints: <ul><li>Rahul is to the left of Murali.</li><li>Srinivas is to the right of Arul.</li></ul> Fix Murali at East \(\Rightarrow\) Rahul at North (left of Murali). Remaining seats South and West for Srinivas and Arul. Srinivas to the right of Arul: \[\text{Arul = South} \Rightarrow \text{right = West} \Rightarrow \text{Srinivas = West}\ \checkmark\] Final arrangement: \[\text{North = Rahul},\quad \text{East = Murali},\quad \text{South = Arul},\quad \text{West = Srinivas}\] <img src="img/d30cf094c033b293.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,269px)"> Opposite pairs: \[\textcolor{red}{\text{Rahul (N)} \longleftrightarrow \text{Arul (S)}}
\qquad
\textcolor{blue}{\text{Srinivas (W)} \longleftrightarrow \text{Murali (E)}}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) Srinivas and Murali}}\]

The slant height of the cone is the distance from the apex to the base edge. Using the given base radius \(r = 5\,\text{cm}\) and height \(h = 12\,\text{cm}\): \[l = \sqrt{r^{2} + h^{2}}
      = \sqrt{5^{2} + 12^{2}}
      = \sqrt{25 + 144}
      = \sqrt{169}
      = 13\,\text{cm}\] When the cone rolls on the ground with point P fixed, the apex traces a circle of radius \(l = 13\,\text{cm}\). The cone returns to its original position after the base circle (circumference \(2\pi r = 10\pi\)) has rolled out completely. Number of rotations of the cone's axis: \[n = \frac{2\pi l}{2\pi r} = \frac{l}{r} = \frac{13}{5}\] Angle swept by the cone (in radians): \[\theta = n \times 2\pi
           = \frac{13}{5} \times 2\pi
           = \frac{26\pi}{5}\] The question asks for the angle the cone travels about P (the swept angle at the apex). The base circumference \(= 2\pi r = 10\pi\) must equal the arc length swept: \[\text{Arc length} = l \times \theta
    \implies 10\pi = 13\,\theta
    \implies \theta = \frac{10\pi}{13}\] \[\boxed{ \text D) \dfrac{10\pi}{13}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Slant height \(l = \sqrt{r^{2} + h^{2}}\).</li><li>When a cone rolls without slipping, the arc swept on the ground equals the base circumference: \(l\,\theta = 2\pi r\).</li><li>Angle travelled \(\theta = \dfrac{2\pi r}{l}\).</li></ul></div>

Evaluate \(\dfrac{(x+y) - |x-y|}{2}\). <strong>Case 1:</strong> \(x \geq y \Rightarrow |x-y| = x-y\): \[\frac{(x+y)-(x-y)}{2}
    = \frac{2y}{2}
    = y
    = \min(x,y)\] <strong>Case 2:</strong> \(x &lt; y \Rightarrow |x-y| = y-x\): \[\frac{(x+y)-(y-x)}{2}
    = \frac{2x}{2}
    = x
    = \min(x,y)\] In both cases the expression equals \(\min(x,y)\). \[\boxed{\text{B. the minimum of }x\text{ and }y}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Standard identity: \(\min(x,y) = \dfrac{(x+y)-|x-y|}{2}\).</li><li>Similarly: \(\max(x,y) = \dfrac{(x+y)+|x-y|}{2}\).</li></ul></div>

\(X\) is a 30-digit number starting with digit 4 followed by the digit 7. <strong>Number of digits in \(X^{3}\):</strong><br/>The number of digits in any integer \(N\) is \(\lfloor \log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\). \[\text{Digits in } X^{3}
    = \lfloor 3\log_{10} X \rfloor + 1\] Since \(X\) is a 30-digit number: \[10^{29} \leq X &lt; 10^{30}\] \[29 \leq \log_{10} X &lt; 30\] \[87 \leq 3\log_{10} X &lt; 90\] More precisely, \(X\) starts with \(47\ldots\): \[\log_{10} X \approx 29 + \log_{10}(4.7\ldots)
    \approx 29 + 0.672 = 29.672\] \[3\log_{10} X \approx 3 \times 29.672 = 89.016\] \[\text{Digits in } X^{3}
    = \lfloor 89.016 \rfloor + 1
    = 89 + 1
    = 90\] \[\boxed{\text{A) 90 digits}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Number of digits in \(N\) \(= \lfloor \log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\).</li><li>For \(N^{k}\): digits \(= \lfloor k\log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\).</li><li>Use the leading digits to estimate \(\log_{10} X\) precisely.</li></ul></div>

Calculate the total standalone workload for all jobs: <ul><li>Job P: \(2 \times 30 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job Q: \(3 \times 20 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job R: \(1 \times 60 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job S: \(4 \times 15 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li></ul> \[\text{Total Workload} = 240\,\text{min}\] With 2 parallel identical machines: \[\text{Minimum Time}
    = \frac{240}{2} = 120\,\text{min}
    = 2\,\text{hours}\] Optimal allocation: <ul><li><strong>Machine 1:</strong> \(\text{R}+\text{P} = 60+60
          = 120\,\text{min}\)</li><li><strong>Machine 2:</strong> \(\text{Q}+\text{S} = 60+60
          = 120\,\text{min}\)</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{A) }2\,\text{hours}}\]
